.SH NAME
writ \- signed statements about keys
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBwrit\fR [\fB\-\-color\fR \fIWHEN\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]...
.br
\fBwrit\fR \fB\-\-help\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
writ makes and checks signed statements about keys: who is who (names) and who may do what (grants).
.PP
Each person or machine has an identity: a short log of keys with an id that never changes. An identity signs statements with its current key. A separate next key, kept offline, can replace the keys later without changing the id. This is called rotation.
.PP
Statements are small signed items. They are stored and sent as text files called bundles. A bundle is any number of items, in any order, so \fBcat a.writ b.writ > c.writ\fR makes a bundle too.
.PP
To check a statement, you name the identities you trust (usually your own) with \-\-as. writ then looks for a chain of grants from them to the subject. Each grant in a chain can only narrow what the one before it allowed.
