.SH IDENTITIES AND KEYS
An identity is a log of key events. The first event creates the identity, and its hash is the id. Later events, called rotations, replace the keys. The id stays the same.
.PP
Identity keys use SLH\-DSA\-SHAKE\-128s, a signature scheme whose security rests only on hash functions. Each event names the current keys, which sign statements, and commits to the next keys, which can sign only the next rotation. A rotation makes new current keys, and can keep the same next keys, so next keys held by friends or kept in a safe serve for many rotations. A thief who steals the current key can't rotate the identity, and a rotation makes everything the thief signed invalid for every verifier that sees it.
.PP
An identity can have several keys, of which K must sign, and K must be more than half of them. This allows identities shared by a group, and recovery with the help of friends.
.SH STATEMENTS AND BUNDLES
There are three kinds of statements. A name says who a name refers to. A grant gives a tag to a subject. A revocation cancels an earlier statement or data signature by the same identity. Names and grants may expire. A statement is identified by the hash of its contents; its signatures are not part of the hash.
.PP
Items are stored as armored text: blocks between \-\-\-\-\-BEGIN WRIT\-\-\-\-\- and \-\-\-\-\-END WRIT\-\-\-\-\- lines. A file of such blocks is a bundle. \fBwrit show\fR prints any bundle in readable form.
.SH TAGS
A tag is a list of words, from general to specific: (ssh), (ssh nas), (zuko home relay). A tag covers itself and every longer tag that starts with it. The empty tag () covers everything. There are no wildcards, and words are compared exactly: (ssh nas) does not cover (ssh nasa).
.PP
Along a chain of grants, the subject holds the longest of the tags, if each tag starts with the one before it. If two tags in the chain differ, the chain grants nothing.
.SH NAMES
Names are local to the identity that makes them. The name path 'me alice laptops' means: whoever I call alice, then whoever she calls laptops. A name can refer to several subjects. A grant to a name path lets the identities along the path decide who gets the grant.
.SH LEAF KEYS
A leaf key is a key without a log. It uses ML\-DSA\-65 and Ed25519 together, and both signatures must be valid. Leaf keys sign and verify quickly, and every grant to a leaf key, or name for one, expires within 24 hours. Use them for machines that sign often. A signature by a leaf key verifies only while the leaf key's grant is live.
.SH TIME
Each statement includes the time it was issued, as given by the signer, and may carry an expiry time. writ does not accept a statement issued more than 5 minutes in the future.
.PP
writ reads the clock, but never uses a time before its build date, or, with \-\-state, before the newest statement seen from the \-\-as identities. So setting the clock back does not make expired grants valid again. Setting it forward only makes grants expire early.
.SH SECRET KEYS
A secret key file holds one line per key, starting with WRIT\-SECRET\-KEY\-1, after a line starting with # that says what the key is and which identity it belongs to. The file is not encrypted. writ creates key files that only their owner can read, and never overwrites a file: a path to write to must not exist yet. Commands that read keys accept \- (standard input) and /dev/fd/N. Commands that write keys or other files accept \- (standard output) and /dev/fd/N, which writ opens for appending, so keys can stay encrypted with age(1):
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
writ grant \-i me \-\-key <(age \-d me.key.age) alice '(ssh)'
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
writ has no network protocol. Verifiers learn about rotations and revocations only from the bundles you give them. A verifier that never sees your rotation keeps trusting your old current key, including in the hands of a thief. \-\-max\-validity does not change that: a thief who has the key can keep making new statements. Give verifiers your newest bundle, and have them use \-\-state, so that once they see a rotation or a revocation they never go back. The same goes for proofs: writ prove output holds only what its sender chose, so check it with \-\-state.
.PP
If two different rotations of one identity appear, writ refuses the identity: someone who holds its next keys signed both. writ keeps refusing it (with \-\-state) until you remove its file from the state directory.
